<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>The numbers of passengers on <m>{5}</m> matatu trips on Thika Road were</p><p style="text-align:center;">24, 28, 14, 27, 27</p><p>Divide by <m>n</m>, the number of values.</p><p>(a) Find the mean, <m>\bar{x}</m>.</p><div><m>\bar{x} =</m> <fillin characters="6" name="ans1"/>  </div><p>(b) Find the variance, <m>\sigma^2</m>, correct to 2 decimal places.</p><div><m>\sigma^2 =</m> <fillin characters="7" name="ans2"/>  </div><p>(c) Find the standard deviation, <m>\sigma</m>, correct to 2 decimal places.</p><div><m>\sigma =</m> <fillin characters="7" name="ans3"/>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>The deviations from the mean always add to zero, so we square them. The variance is the mean of the squared deviations from the mean, and the standard deviation is the square root of the variance, in the units of the data.</p><p>(a) <m>\bar{x} = \dfrac{\Sigma x}{n} = \dfrac{ {120} }{ {5} } = {24}</m>.</p><p>(b) By deviations: the values of <m>x - \bar{x}</m> are 0, 4, -10, 3, 3, and their squares add to <m>\Sigma (x - \bar{x})^2 = {134}</m>. So<me>\sigma^2 = \frac{\Sigma (x - \bar{x})^2}{n} = \frac{ {134} }{ {5} } \approx {26.80}</me>Or by the second form: <m>\Sigma x^2 = {3014}</m>, so <m>\sigma^2 = \dfrac{ {3014} }{ {5} } - {24}^2</m>, the same value.</p><p>(c) <m>\sigma = \sqrt{\sigma^2} \approx {5.18}</m>. Use the unrounded variance when you take the square root.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>24</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{134}{5}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{\sqrt{134}}{\sqrt{5}}</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>